1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,000
The Girl Was Scared of the Wilderness

2
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:14,000
The girl was scared of the wilderness, her heart pounding at every frightening noise.

3
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:20,000
But suddenly her fear disappeared. She touched the ground, and it felt like her new guardian.

4
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:22,000
What's wrong with this picture?

5
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:29,000
Well, for one, this passage doesn't actually paint much of a picture, and it fails to make me feel anything as a reader.

6
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:34,000
That's the core problem with writing that relies too much on telling.

7
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:40,000
We're told the girl is scared, that the noises are frightening, and that this place feels like a guardian.

8
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:43,000
Yet there isn't much evidence to back up those claims.

9
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:47,000
Show-don't-tell is a phrase you've probably heard often in the writing community.

10
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:50,000
Author K.M. Weiland best captures the distinction.

11
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:54,000
Showing dramatizes, telling summarizes.

12
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:58,000
But it can be hard to identify harmful instances of telling in your own writing.

13
00:00:58,000 --> 00:00:59,000
Telling is a form of self-reliance.

14
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:01,000
Telling is not inherently bad.

15
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:04,000
In fact, all novels are a blend of telling and showing.

16
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:07,000
You don't always need to show instead of tell.

17
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:12,000
If that were the case, all stories would be ridiculously long and filled with unnecessary descriptions.

18
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:19,000
Telling is useful for quickly conveying the passage of time or presenting important facts to the reader without belaboring the point.

19
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:25,000
Take a look at the opening of the children's novel The Secret Garden by Frances Hodgson Burnett.

20
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:28,000
When Mary Lennox was sent to Misselthwaite Manor

21
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:34,000
to live with her uncle, everybody said she was the most disagreeable-looking child ever seen.

22
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:36,000
It was true, too.

23
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:43,000
She had a little thin face and a little thin body, thin light hair and a sour expression.

24
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:51,000
Her hair was yellow and her face was yellow because she had been born in India and had always been ill in one way or another.

25
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:57,000
We're told some information about Mary being a disagreeable-looking child who's always been ill.

26
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:04,000
But that statement is immediately supported by the visual proof of her thin face and hair, along with her sour expression.

27
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:13,000
If someone flags your writing for too much telling, that likely means you need to provide details or a strong narrative voice to make the reader feel something.

28
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:20,000
Good writing invites the reader to visualize the scene and experience the emotions themselves, rather than being told how to feel.

29
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:25,000
In his TED Talk, The Clues to a Great Story, Pixar writer and director Andrew Stanton proposes the uniformity of the story.

30
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:26,000
The story is a story of a man and a woman.

31
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:29,000
The story proposes the unifying theory of two plus two.

32
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:30,000
He says,

33
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:32,000
Make the audience put things together.

34
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:35,000
Don't give them four. Give them two plus two.

35
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:36,000
The opening of

36
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:43,000
relies entirely on showing the audience the equation without giving them the answer.

37
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:46,000
And he describes why that approach is effective.

38
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:51,000
Storytelling without dialogue, it's the purest form of cinematic storytelling.

39
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:54,000
It's the most inclusive approach you can take.

40
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:56,000
It confirms something I really haven't seen before.

41
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:00,000
In the end, the audience wants to work for their meal.

42
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:03,000
They just don't want to know they are doing that.

43
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:09,000
That's your job as the storyteller, to hide the fact that you are making them work for their meal.

44
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:11,000
We are born problem solvers.

45
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:15,000
We are compelled to deduce and to deduct.

46
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:17,000
That's what we do in real life.

47
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:22,000
It is this well-organized absence of information that draws us in.

48
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:25,000
There is a reason we are all attracted to an infant or a puppy.

49
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:32,000
It's not just that they're damn cute. It's because they can't completely express what they're thinking and what their intentions are.

50
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:38,000
And it's like a magnet. We can't stop ourselves from wanting to complete the sentence and fill it in.

51
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:43,000
The same principle is true for other non-visual modes of storytelling.

52
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:49,000
The key to making your audience care in fiction is to imply your meaning, rather than always pointing it out.

53
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:53,000
Readers love the process of discovery and solving puzzles.

54
00:03:53,000 --> 00:04:04,000
Before we dive into some practical strategies, I want to take a two-minute detour to explore the origins of Show, Don't Tell as a writing mantra, since it's not often discussed.

55
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:17,000
In his 2004 book Creative Writing and the New Humanities, scholar Paul Dawson describes how the novel transformed across the 19th and 20th centuries with the rise of realism as a literary movement.

56
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:23,000
Realism aims to tell the stories of ordinary people with complete honesty, rather than romanticizing them.

57
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:25,000
Dawson notes,

58
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:51,000
Literary critic Percy Lubbock praised those 19th century realists for giving the novel a defined aesthetic, and it's his 1921 book The Craft of Fiction that likely popularized the idea of showing versus telling.

59
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:52,000
He says,

60
00:04:53,000 --> 00:05:02,000
The art of fiction does not begin until the novelist thinks of his story as a matter to be shown, to be so exhibited that it will tell itself.

61
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:09,000
Authors like Virginia Woolf both praised and criticized Lubbock for confining the novel writing craft to a formal system.

62
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:13,000
His ideas were incredibly influential in the literary world.

63
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:21,000
Basically, the intention of this advice is to turn the author into an invisible narrator, and avoid breaking the reader's immersion in the story.

64
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:27,000
As Hardy makes a similar observation in her 2016 writing guide Understanding Show, Don't Tell,

65
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:29,000
A common rule of thumb.

66
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:32,000
As long as it feels like the character is thinking it, you're usually okay.

67
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:38,000
But as soon as it sounds like the author butting in to explain things, you've probably fallen into telling.

68
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:44,000
If you want readers to experience the story's emotions on a gut level, you've got to know when to show.

69
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:50,000
Generally speaking, moments involving emotions, opinions, or sensations are best shown rather than told.

70
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:54,000
Here are six guiding principles for stronger showing.

71
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:58,000
1. Use evidence to support your claims

72
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:07,000
If a narrator says her husband is a kind-hearted person, or the protagonist believes his best friend is guilty of murder, what led them to that conclusion?

73
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:13,000
Give the reader the same evidence the character uses when it comes to assumptions or opinions.

74
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:19,000
Author Chuck Pumpernickel advises a ban on thought verbs like thinks, knows, understands, realizes, and thinks.

75
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:20,000
Author Chuck Pumpernickel advises a ban on thought verbs like thinks, knows, understands, realizes, and thinks.

76
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:23,000
Author Chuck Pumpernickel advises a ban on thought verbs like thinks, knows, understands, believes, wants, remembers, and imagines.

77
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:24,000
Author Chuck Pumpernickel advises a ban on thought verbs like thinks, knows, understands, believes, wants, remembers, and imagines.

78
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:29,000
He talks about unpacking scenes so that the reader feels and thinks what the characters are feeling and thinking.

79
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:31,000
He gives this example.

80
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:32,000
Instead of saying,

81
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:34,000
Adam knew Gwen liked him,

82
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:35,000
you'll have to say,

83
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:40,000
Between classes, Gwen was always leaned on his locker when he'd go to open it.

84
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:44,000
She'd roll her eyes and shove off of one foot, leaving a black heel mark on the painted metal.

85
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:47,000
But she also loved the smell of her perfume.

86
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:49,000
The combination lock would still be warm from her eyes.

87
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:54,000
And the next break, Gwen would be leaned there again.

88
00:06:54,000 --> 00:06:55,000
Pumpernickel adds,

89
00:06:55,000 --> 00:07:01,000
Instead of characters knowing anything, you must now present the details that allow the reader to know them.

90
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:07,000
Instead of a character wanting something, you must now describe the thing so that the reader wants it.

91
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:11,000
You can pull readers into the story by presenting evidence,

92
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:14,000
whether that's a visual detail or a piece of dialogue,

93
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:18,000
and letting them come to their own conclusions about the impression you're trying to create.

94
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:20,000
You can also tell the story by presenting evidence,

95
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:22,000
or by presenting evidence that you're trying to create.

96
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:25,000
Number 2. Replace the abstract with the concrete.

97
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:30,000
In particular, be careful about directly stating a character's feelings.

98
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:34,000
The blog Novel Writing Help by Harvey Chapman gives a great before and after example.

99
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:35,000
Telling,

100
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:37,000
After his first kiss with Samantha,

101
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:42,000
Toby walked home feeling happier than he'd ever felt in all his 13 years.

102
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:43,000
Showing,

103
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:45,000
After his first kiss with Samantha,

104
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:48,000
Toby couldn't keep the goofy grin off his face all the way home.

105
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:51,000
He jumped clean over it, didn't come close to tripping.

106
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:54,000
Chapman further explains why the changes work.

107
00:07:54,000 --> 00:07:59,000
Happiness is an abstract concept and needs to be demonstrated

108
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:03,000
with concrete details .

109
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:09,000
So, you can often replace emotions with actions that allow the reader to infer the emotion.

110
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:14,000
Also, be wary of descriptions that use opinion-related adjectives, like beautiful or strange.

111
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:16,000
In a first draft, I might write,

112
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:18,000
The dark forest felt eerie.

113
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:22,000
Okay, maybe it feels eerie to the character, but the reader needs to feel it too.

114
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:25,000
I need to convince the reader that it's eerie using evidence.

115
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:29,000
The forest hummed with the cries of children long dead.

116
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:31,000
That's better. Sufficiently eerie.

117
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:36,000
Replace adjective labels with details that allow the reader to interpret the atmosphere on their own.

118
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:44,000
A trick for identifying when you're in abstract territory is to ask a question that Jeff Gerk poses in his book The First 50 Pages.

119
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:46,000
Can the camera see it?

120
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:48,000
Almost all examples of showing contain a question.

121
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:51,000
It's a great way to contain a detail that can be visualized,

122
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:55,000
although you'll often want to combine those visuals with smell, touch, taste, and sound.

123
00:08:55,000 --> 00:09:02,000
Author Jerry Jenkins provides great examples of replacing abstract emotions with concrete actions on his blog.

124
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:04,000
Cold? Don't tell me, show me.

125
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:09,000
Your character pulls her collar up, tightens her scarf, shoves her hands deep into her pockets,

126
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:11,000
turns her face away from the biting wind.

127
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:14,000
Tired? He can yawn, groan, stretch.

128
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:16,000
His eyes can look puffy. His shoulders could slump.

129
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:17,000
Another character might say,

130
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:20,000
Didn't you sleep last night? You look shot.

131
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:27,000
Another way of thinking about this camera idea is to consider the effect and not the cause of a particular detail.

132
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:31,000
Take a look at these additional examples from Jerry Jenkins.

133
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:34,000
The temperature fell and the ice reflected the sun.

134
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:40,000
Bill's nose burned in the frigid air and he squinted against the sun reflecting off the street.

135
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:43,000
Susie was blind.

136
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:46,000
Susie felt for the bench with a white cane.

137
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:48,000
It was late fall.

138
00:09:48,000 --> 00:09:50,000
Leaves crunched beneath his feet.

139
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:55,000
In the first example, the cold temperature is the cause of specific effects on the character,

140
00:09:55,000 --> 00:09:58,000
namely Bill's nose burning in the frigid air.

141
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:04,000
Instead of being stated outright, the details are instead shown through how the character interacts with the world around them,

142
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:09,000
like leaves crunching beneath their feet, which makes the scene more visual.

143
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:14,000
Substitute vague descriptions with specific sensory details.

144
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:17,000
Above all, showing relies on specificity.

145
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:21,000
Unique sensory details make feelings and scenes jump off the page.

146
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:26,000
Author Delilah Dawson talks about invoking the senses to make the world-building feel three-dimensional.

147
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:29,000
In her first draft of a sentence, she writes,

148
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:33,000
Aga walked through the market, gaping at the rugs and bins of spices.

149
00:10:33,000 --> 00:10:35,000
Now, she could have said,

150
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:38,000
Aga marveled at all the market's wondrous sights.

151
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:42,000
But instead of using abstract concepts like marveled or wondrous,

152
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:47,000
she includes the concrete action of Aga gaping at the rugs and bins of spices.

153
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:51,000
But even though that creates a mental image, it's not very specific,

154
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:54,000
and it doesn't invoke any senses beyond sight.

155
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:56,000
In her second draft, Dawson writes,

156
00:10:56,000 --> 00:10:59,000
Aga walked through the market as if through a dream.

157
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:05,000
Spicy cinnamon and rich coffee rode the air as she ran her fingers down silken tassels

158
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:08,000
and through powdery barrels of golden saffron.

159
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:11,000
Adding details makes this description feel much more immersive,

160
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:14,000
and it's unique to this particular story.

161
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:18,000
When showing details, try to go beyond the obvious and expect it.

162
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:23,000
For instance, a funeral scene will often show everyone wearing black as it begins to rain,

163
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:27,000
the main character standing with an umbrella in front of her mother's grave.

164
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:31,000
What if you showed details that contrasted with the somber atmosphere?

165
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:34,000
If an emotional scene feels too cliché,

166
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:38,000
try changing the setting or the way the characters describe their emotions,

167
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:40,000
as author Gail Carson Levine recommends.

168
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:46,000
What if, instead of an ordinary day, it's Christmas Day in southern Texas?

169
00:11:46,000 --> 00:11:48,000
Begone drizzle, hello dry air.

170
00:11:48,000 --> 00:11:52,000
What if the gravestone has something written on it that doesn't make sense to anyone,

171
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:57,000
but was requested by her dying mother to be engraved on her tombstone?

172
00:11:57,000 --> 00:12:00,000
Maybe the friend asked the main character what it means.

173
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:04,000
Maybe they take their minds off the sadness by trying to figure out the odd saying.

174
00:12:04,000 --> 00:12:08,000
The emotional scene is no longer cheesy because it's different.

175
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:09,000
Number 4.

176
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:10,000
Avoid relying too much on the story.

177
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:13,000
Avoid relying too much on body language.

178
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:17,000
A lot of writing advice suggests using body language to imply a character's emotions.

179
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:20,000
Crashed arms might show that someone is pissed off,

180
00:12:20,000 --> 00:12:23,000
whereas tapping fingers can indicate impatience.

181
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:26,000
Those physical details can make for good emotional shorthand.

182
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:30,000
However, it's easy to over-rely on body language as a form of showing.

183
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:36,000
In real life, how often do you see someone clench their fist or grit their teeth when they're angry?

184
00:12:36,000 --> 00:12:38,000
How often have you done that yourself when you're angry?

185
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:43,000
Common facial expressions and gestures are great for quickly conveying a character's mood,

186
00:12:43,000 --> 00:12:47,000
but they rarely evoke an emotional response from the reader.

187
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:51,000
In an article on C.S. Lakin's blog Live Write Thrive,

188
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:56,000
editor Robin Patchen describes how writers can show emotions through actions and thoughts,

189
00:12:56,000 --> 00:12:59,000
rather than bodily sensations alone.

190
00:12:59,000 --> 00:13:00,000
As she says,

191
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:03,000
Having a character clenching his fist might show us he's angry,

192
00:13:03,000 --> 00:13:06,000
but it doesn't show us the impetus for that anger.

193
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:07,000
Is he feeling frustrated, sluggish, or angry?

194
00:13:07,000 --> 00:13:09,000
Is he feeling frustrated, slighted, or jealous?

195
00:13:09,000 --> 00:13:12,000
She gives an amazing before and after example.

196
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:16,000
The first version relies heavily on body language.

197
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:19,000
Mary opened her eyes and looked at the clock.

198
00:13:19,000 --> 00:13:21,000
Her heart nearly leapt out of her chest.

199
00:13:21,000 --> 00:13:23,000
The baby had slept nearly eight hours.

200
00:13:23,000 --> 00:13:26,000
But little Jane never slept more than four hours at a time.

201
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:28,000
Something must be wrong.

202
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:29,000
Not again.

203
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:33,000
Her stomach rolled over when she remembered the last time a child of hers had slept too long.

204
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:37,000
At first glance, it seems like the story is showing the character's emotions,

205
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:39,000
because her heart and stomach are reacting.

206
00:13:39,000 --> 00:13:44,000
But that same lack of subtlety makes the descriptions feel forced and melodramatic.

207
00:13:44,000 --> 00:13:49,000
Patchen's second version of this scene moves away from visceral reactions

208
00:13:49,000 --> 00:13:52,000
and focuses on the character's individual thought process.

209
00:13:52,000 --> 00:13:58,000
Mary opened her eyes and squinted in the sunshine streaming in through the open window.

210
00:13:58,000 --> 00:14:01,000
She stretched, feeling more relaxed than she had since.

211
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:03,000
She sat up and looked at the clock.

212
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:04,000
It was after eight.

213
00:14:04,000 --> 00:14:06,000
Little Jane had slept through the night.

214
00:14:07,000 --> 00:14:08,000
For the first time.

215
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:10,000
Just like Billy.

216
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:12,000
Mary flipped the covers back and stood.

217
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:15,000
She snatched her robe from the back of the chair and slipped it on.

218
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:16,000
She wouldn't think about Billy.

219
00:14:16,000 --> 00:14:18,000
The doctor said it wouldn't happen again.

220
00:14:18,000 --> 00:14:20,000
The odds against it were astronomical.

221
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:22,000
Billy had been nearly six weeks old.

222
00:14:22,000 --> 00:14:24,000
Jane was almost two months.

223
00:14:24,000 --> 00:14:25,000
It was different this time.

224
00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:26,000
It had to be.

225
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:29,000
The second example feels more in the moment.

226
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:32,000
Giving a real-time account of the character's thought process

227
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:36,000
and their interactions with the setting can show emotional nuance better than body language.

228
00:14:36,000 --> 00:14:40,000
Patchen also uses strong verbs like flipped and snatched

229
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:43,000
to convey a sense of panic and urgency,

230
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:46,000
along with the ellipsis that indicates her thoughts trailing off.

231
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:49,000
Word choice and sentence structure can be a form of showing.

232
00:14:49,000 --> 00:14:53,000
Robin Patchen ends with this beautiful nugget of wisdom.

233
00:14:53,000 --> 00:14:56,000
Counselors tell us that thoughts lead to emotions,

234
00:14:56,000 --> 00:14:58,000
and emotions lead to actions.

235
00:14:58,000 --> 00:15:01,000
As a writer, you can easily show your characters' thoughts and actions.

236
00:15:01,000 --> 00:15:04,000
Readers are smart enough to deduce the emotions

237
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:06,000
based on what the characters think and do.

238
00:15:06,000 --> 00:15:09,000
So often it seems writers are in a hurry.

239
00:15:09,000 --> 00:15:12,000
When you have a very emotional scene, slow it down.

240
00:15:12,000 --> 00:15:14,000
Let us hear your characters' every thought.

241
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:16,000
Highlight a few details.

242
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:18,000
Show the actions.

243
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:21,000
If you need help brainstorming how feelings might manifest,

244
00:15:21,000 --> 00:15:25,000
check out The Emotion Thesaurus by Angela Ackerman and Becca Puglisi,

245
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:29,000
which lists a variety of ways you can convey different emotions,

246
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:31,000
from anguish to wanderlust.

247
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:33,000
Number five.

248
00:15:33,000 --> 00:15:35,000
Show emotion through dialogue.

249
00:15:35,000 --> 00:15:38,000
Dialogue is a powerful tool for proving a character's feelings

250
00:15:38,000 --> 00:15:40,000
or personality to the reader.

251
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:41,000
Instead of saying,

252
00:15:41,000 --> 00:15:42,000
Mary was angry at Bob,

253
00:15:42,000 --> 00:15:44,000
you could have Mary shout at Bob,

254
00:15:44,000 --> 00:15:46,000
You wretched pickle-monger, how dare you!

255
00:15:46,000 --> 00:15:49,000
This is also the reason many writing advice articles

256
00:15:49,000 --> 00:15:51,000
warn against using adverbs.

257
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:55,000
They weaken the dialogue because they tell rather than show.

258
00:15:55,000 --> 00:15:58,000
In the above dialogue, we can tell Mary's tone from her words alone,

259
00:15:58,000 --> 00:16:01,000
not to mention the volume, given she's shouting.

260
00:16:01,000 --> 00:16:03,000
I don't need to write shouted angrily

261
00:16:03,000 --> 00:16:05,000
because the word angrily is a word

262
00:16:05,000 --> 00:16:08,000
that's telling the reader information we've already shown.

263
00:16:08,000 --> 00:16:11,000
Similarly, some writers feel tempted to telegraph

264
00:16:11,000 --> 00:16:13,000
a character's intentions in a conversation,

265
00:16:13,000 --> 00:16:16,000
even though the dialogue already shows that information.

266
00:16:16,000 --> 00:16:18,000
He tried to be diplomatic.

267
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:20,000
Please just listen to what I have to say.

268
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:23,000
Well, that doesn't matter, she said, changing the subject.

269
00:16:23,000 --> 00:16:25,000
Let's move on to something else.

270
00:16:25,000 --> 00:16:27,000
In this exchange, the author is telling the reader

271
00:16:27,000 --> 00:16:29,000
what conclusions to make,

272
00:16:29,000 --> 00:16:31,000
when they should trust that their readers are smart enough

273
00:16:31,000 --> 00:16:33,000
to figure it out on their own.

274
00:16:33,000 --> 00:16:35,000
The author's tone might include more visuals

275
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:38,000
and a dialogue tag that conveys a specific tone.

276
00:16:38,000 --> 00:16:40,000
He pinched the bridge of his nose.

277
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:42,000
Please, just listen to what I have to say.

278
00:16:42,000 --> 00:16:44,000
Well, that doesn't matter, she whispered.

279
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:46,000
Let's move on to something else.

280
00:16:46,000 --> 00:16:49,000
When writing highly emotional dialogue,

281
00:16:49,000 --> 00:16:51,000
it might be helpful in the first draft

282
00:16:51,000 --> 00:16:53,000
to pretend you're writing a play or screenplay,

283
00:16:53,000 --> 00:16:55,000
since that forces you to focus on conveying emotion

284
00:16:55,000 --> 00:16:57,000
through dialogue alone.

285
00:16:57,000 --> 00:17:00,000
Oscar Wilde is known for his snappy dialogue,

286
00:17:00,000 --> 00:17:02,000
particularly in his play The Importance of Brute.

287
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:05,000
In the opening scene, a young gentleman named Algernon

288
00:17:05,000 --> 00:17:07,000
visits with his best friend Jack,

289
00:17:07,000 --> 00:17:09,000
who has come to propose to Algernon's cousin.

290
00:17:09,000 --> 00:17:12,000
The dialogue carries the emotions of the scene.

291
00:17:32,000 --> 00:17:35,000
In the second place, I don't give my consent.

292
00:17:35,000 --> 00:17:36,000
Your consent!

293
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:39,000
My dear fellow Gwendolyn is my first cousin.

294
00:17:39,000 --> 00:17:41,000
Based on the tone of their words alone,

295
00:17:41,000 --> 00:17:43,000
the reader can assume what the characters are feeling,

296
00:17:43,000 --> 00:17:46,000
even though the audience isn't told that information directly.

297
00:17:46,000 --> 00:17:49,000
You can learn more about writing subtext and dialogue

298
00:17:49,000 --> 00:17:51,000
in my video on the subject.

299
00:17:51,000 --> 00:17:55,000
Number 6. Filter observations through narrative voice.

300
00:17:55,000 --> 00:17:57,000
Show-don't-tell often means going deeper

301
00:17:57,000 --> 00:17:59,000
into the narrative point of view.

302
00:17:59,000 --> 00:18:01,000
Whether you're filtering the story through the lens

303
00:18:01,000 --> 00:18:03,000
of a character or a more distant narrator,

304
00:18:03,000 --> 00:18:05,000
it's about giving details that allow the reader

305
00:18:05,000 --> 00:18:08,000
to feel more connected to the point of view character

306
00:18:08,000 --> 00:18:10,000
through what they're experiencing.

307
00:18:10,000 --> 00:18:12,000
This closeness can be achieved through phrasing

308
00:18:12,000 --> 00:18:14,000
straightforward statements in a unique way.

309
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:18,000
Reddit user Chevron7Loc shares some great examples

310
00:18:18,000 --> 00:18:21,000
of telling versus showing on the R Writing subreddit.

311
00:18:21,000 --> 00:18:24,000
He was a rude and inconsiderate man.

312
00:18:24,000 --> 00:18:25,000
This is telling.

313
00:18:25,000 --> 00:18:27,000
We know the character is rude and inconsiderate

314
00:18:27,000 --> 00:18:28,000
because the writer told us.

315
00:18:28,000 --> 00:18:30,000
Out of my way, you jerk!

316
00:18:30,000 --> 00:18:33,000
He yelled at the woman struggling to lift her stroller onto the bus.

317
00:18:33,000 --> 00:18:34,000
This is showing.

318
00:18:34,000 --> 00:18:37,000
We can deduce that the character is rude and inconsiderate

319
00:18:37,000 --> 00:18:40,000
based on the situation we just read.

320
00:18:40,000 --> 00:18:41,000
Telling.

321
00:18:41,000 --> 00:18:43,000
She was uncomfortable around him.

322
00:18:43,000 --> 00:18:44,000
Showing.

323
00:18:44,000 --> 00:18:46,000
She stiffened in his embrace.

324
00:18:46,000 --> 00:18:47,000
Telling.

325
00:18:47,000 --> 00:18:49,000
The house was huge.

326
00:18:49,000 --> 00:18:50,000
Showing.

327
00:18:50,000 --> 00:18:53,000
His whole family could live in the kitchen alone.

328
00:18:53,000 --> 00:18:54,000
Telling.

329
00:18:54,000 --> 00:18:55,000
She was hungry.

330
00:18:55,000 --> 00:18:56,000
Showing.

331
00:18:56,000 --> 00:18:58,000
She near inhaled the soup.

332
00:18:58,000 --> 00:19:00,000
In all of these examples, we learn the same information

333
00:19:00,000 --> 00:19:03,000
through showing but with more flavor and character.

334
00:19:03,000 --> 00:19:07,000
You'll notice that all these examples involve replacing was

335
00:19:07,000 --> 00:19:11,000
with a more interesting verb, just as in earlier examples.

336
00:19:11,000 --> 00:19:15,000
Since was and its cousin word felt are often followed by an adjective,

337
00:19:15,000 --> 00:19:18,000
that can be a flag marking a spot where a stronger verb could be used

338
00:19:18,000 --> 00:19:21,000
to create that concrete image in the reader's head.

339
00:19:21,000 --> 00:19:24,000
You can picture a woman stiffening in a man's embrace

340
00:19:24,000 --> 00:19:26,000
or a kitchen large enough for a family to live in

341
00:19:26,000 --> 00:19:28,000
or someone inhaling soup.

342
00:19:28,000 --> 00:19:30,000
This extends to world-building,

343
00:19:30,000 --> 00:19:32,000
backstory, and info dumps in general.

344
00:19:32,000 --> 00:19:36,000
Sometimes authors present information like a dictionary definition

345
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:40,000
rather than an in-world reference that fits naturally into the story.

346
00:19:40,000 --> 00:19:41,000
As Janice Hardy notes,

347
00:19:41,000 --> 00:19:46,000
An easy test for info dumps is to check if the information is for the reader's benefit

348
00:19:46,000 --> 00:19:48,000
or the character's benefit.

349
00:19:48,000 --> 00:19:53,000
If it's for the reader, chances are you're dumping and it contains told prose.

350
00:19:53,000 --> 00:19:57,000
The key is to filter the world-building or exposition through the point-of-view character's perspective.

351
00:19:57,000 --> 00:20:00,000
Hardy compares different ways of showing the same scene

352
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:02,000
based on the character.

353
00:20:02,000 --> 00:20:04,000
The bland example was Bob.

354
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:07,000
The rain poured down the window of the restaurant.

355
00:20:07,000 --> 00:20:11,000
Bob sat at the table, a stack of pancakes beside him.

356
00:20:11,000 --> 00:20:13,000
He stared at an envelope in his hands.

357
00:20:13,000 --> 00:20:16,000
While above him on the wall, a clock ticked.

358
00:20:16,000 --> 00:20:18,000
A Navy Seal character.

359
00:20:18,000 --> 00:20:21,000
The rain beat against the restaurant window like rounds from an Uzi.

360
00:20:21,000 --> 00:20:24,000
Bob sat at the table, back against the wall,

361
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:26,000
a stack of uneaten pancakes beside him.

362
00:20:26,000 --> 00:20:29,000
He gripped the envelope tighter with every tick of the clock above him.

363
00:20:29,000 --> 00:20:31,000
New orders. Great.

364
00:20:31,000 --> 00:20:33,000
A scared girl.

365
00:20:33,000 --> 00:20:37,000
Rain covered the window and blurred the outside world.

366
00:20:37,000 --> 00:20:41,000
Bob slouched at the table, her head barely higher than the stack of pancakes beside her.

367
00:20:41,000 --> 00:20:43,000
The envelope lay in her lap.

368
00:20:43,000 --> 00:20:45,000
She didn't want to touch it, let alone open it.

369
00:20:45,000 --> 00:20:47,000
She glanced at the clock and sighed,

370
00:20:47,000 --> 00:20:48,000
running out of time.

371
00:20:48,000 --> 00:20:52,000
Let your character's emotions color the way they see their surroundings.

372
00:20:52,000 --> 00:20:56,000
The same goes for dialogue that feels like information given for the reader's sake,

373
00:20:56,000 --> 00:20:58,000
rather than something the character would realistically say.

374
00:20:58,000 --> 00:21:06,000
This leads to, as you know Bob, situations where one character explains something that another character already knows.

375
00:21:06,000 --> 00:21:12,000
No! No, you know how today we're heading into the land of the giants to offer them the Drew Valencia in exchange for joining our quest to save Princess Isabella?

376
00:21:12,000 --> 00:21:17,000
Yes, we discussed it last night in great detail. There's no need for your clunky exposition.

377
00:21:17,000 --> 00:21:22,000
In these situations, the dialogue isn't phrased in the character's voice.

378
00:21:22,000 --> 00:21:26,000
It's the author talking instead, which pulls the reader out of the story.

379
00:21:26,000 --> 00:21:31,360
Remember that characters have prior knowledge and experience that exist outside of the narrative.

380
00:21:31,360 --> 00:21:36,000
Showing in dialogue often means including less detail, as in this example from Janice Hardy.

381
00:21:36,640 --> 00:21:37,760
Reader's Benefit

382
00:21:37,760 --> 00:21:40,560
I'll rig up a small explosive device to blow open the door.

383
00:21:40,560 --> 00:21:43,920
That's the way we did it when I was deployed in Afghanistan as a Navy SEAL.

384
00:21:44,480 --> 00:21:46,000
Character's Benefit

385
00:21:46,000 --> 00:21:48,720
Uh, Kevin, where'd you learn to make bombs?

386
00:21:49,280 --> 00:21:49,920
The Navy

387
00:21:50,640 --> 00:21:55,920
The true purpose behind the advice Show Don't Tell is not to assert that all telling is bad writing.

388
00:21:55,920 --> 00:21:58,480
Telling is often necessary to bridge different scenes,

389
00:21:58,480 --> 00:22:03,920
and the amount of telling you use can depend on the genre, which Dewey writes captures so succinctly.

390
00:22:03,920 --> 00:22:06,800
Telling and showing are tools to control pacing.

391
00:22:06,800 --> 00:22:10,720
They help you focus and immerse the reader in important moments and speed past others.

392
00:22:10,720 --> 00:22:12,560
What do you want the reader to remember?

393
00:22:12,560 --> 00:22:15,040
Identify what your ideal reader wants.

394
00:22:15,040 --> 00:22:17,680
Do they want a lush, immersive, meandering journey?

395
00:22:17,680 --> 00:22:19,440
Or a light-speed thriller?

396
00:22:19,440 --> 00:22:21,920
And as author Alexi Harrow puts it,

397
00:22:21,920 --> 00:22:24,720
sometimes readers want to be told what's happening.

398
00:22:24,720 --> 00:22:25,840
As if we're listening to

399
00:22:25,840 --> 00:22:25,920
an

400
00:22:25,920 --> 00:22:28,400
oral storyteller spin a good yarn.

401
00:22:28,400 --> 00:22:29,520
She writes,

402
00:22:29,520 --> 00:22:33,120
I want a strong narrative voice to come sweeping across the stage

403
00:22:33,120 --> 00:22:37,120
in a grand monologue that explains the whole world to me like I'm five.

404
00:22:37,120 --> 00:22:40,640
I want the patronizing clarity of a fairy tale or a myth,

405
00:22:40,640 --> 00:22:44,560
which strings together a story with a series of and thens and untils.

406
00:22:44,560 --> 00:22:49,200
I want a flat southern voice leaning close and saying so what happened was.

407
00:22:50,240 --> 00:22:55,840
Harrow specifically champions Micaiah Johnson's sci-fi debut The Space Between Worlds.

408
00:22:55,840 --> 00:23:01,840
As an example of a work that turns exposition into something searing and compelling with narrative tension,

409
00:23:01,840 --> 00:23:05,760
the opening pages use a strong first-person voice to pull the reader in.

410
00:23:05,760 --> 00:23:12,240
When I was young and multiverse was just a theory, I was worthless.

411
00:23:13,120 --> 00:23:18,080
The brown girl-child of an addict in one of those wards outside the walls of Wiley City

412
00:23:18,080 --> 00:23:20,400
that people don't get out of or go to.

413
00:23:21,280 --> 00:23:25,680
But then Adam Bosch, our new Einstein and the founder of the Institute for the Arts,

414
00:23:25,680 --> 00:23:29,840
the Institute that pays me, discovered a way to see into other universes.

415
00:23:30,800 --> 00:23:33,280
Of course, humanity couldn't just look.

416
00:23:33,280 --> 00:23:34,800
We had to enter.

417
00:23:34,800 --> 00:23:37,920
We had to touch and taste and take.

418
00:23:39,120 --> 00:23:40,800
But the universe said no.

419
00:23:42,000 --> 00:23:47,280
Telling is part of what differentiates novels and short stories from movies and TV shows.

420
00:23:47,280 --> 00:23:52,960
Fiction writers can bottle thoughts and feelings in a way that can't be fully replicated in another medium.

421
00:23:52,960 --> 00:23:55,440
Showing is meant to push writers to try harder

422
00:23:55,440 --> 00:24:00,640
prose and brainstorm specific details that bring the characters and world to life.

423
00:24:00,640 --> 00:24:03,200
That being said, the phrase should be amended to,

424
00:24:03,200 --> 00:24:05,280
Show, don't just tell.

425
00:24:05,280 --> 00:24:10,800
As a quick cheat sheet, here are some places where you might consider telling or blend telling and showing.

426
00:24:11,440 --> 00:24:16,560
Moments unimportant to the larger narrative, like how a character got from point A to point B.

427
00:24:16,560 --> 00:24:20,960
Summaries of routines, time passing, or repetitive conversations.

428
00:24:20,960 --> 00:24:25,440
Some aspects of magic systems or sci-fi worldbuilding, as in the space between worlds.

429
00:24:25,440 --> 00:24:27,440
Character thoughts.

430
00:24:27,440 --> 00:24:29,440
Occasional backstory and exposition.

431
00:24:29,440 --> 00:24:35,440
This is usually presented as a broad sweep that tells paired with specific details that show, like in the Secret Garden example.

432
00:24:35,440 --> 00:24:39,440
And here are places where you're usually better off showing.

433
00:24:39,440 --> 00:24:41,440
Emotions.

434
00:24:41,440 --> 00:24:45,440
Particularly the character's feelings and assumptions about how other characters are feeling.

435
00:24:45,440 --> 00:24:47,440
Sensations.

436
00:24:47,440 --> 00:24:49,440
This includes sights, sounds, smell, taste, and touch.

437
00:24:49,440 --> 00:24:51,440
Thoughts.

438
00:24:51,440 --> 00:24:53,440
Words like realize, thought, and knew might signify this, but don't feel like you need to avoid those words entirely.

439
00:24:53,440 --> 00:24:55,440
Emotions.

440
00:24:55,440 --> 00:24:59,440
Just make sure there's not a more interesting way you could phrase it, using concrete evidence.

441
00:24:59,440 --> 00:25:01,440
Attributes or opinion-related adjectives.

442
00:25:01,440 --> 00:25:07,440
Especially in relation to how a character, place, or situation makes the protagonist feel.

443
00:25:07,440 --> 00:25:13,440
If you make a claim like, he was smart, back it up with evidence, such as mentioning the time he MacGyvered a key from dental floss and a spatula.

444
00:25:13,440 --> 00:25:15,440
Flat phrasing.

445
00:25:15,440 --> 00:25:19,440
This might include overused wording or an abundance of to-be verbs like was.

446
00:25:19,440 --> 00:25:23,440
Showing means eliminating the author as the middleman, and letting the author know that he's the middleman.

447
00:25:23,440 --> 00:25:25,440
Showing means eliminating the author as the middleman, and letting the author know that he's the middleman.

448
00:25:25,440 --> 00:25:27,440
Seeing the reader live the story firsthand.

449
00:25:27,440 --> 00:25:35,440
You can show through specificity, action, dialogue, sensory details, internal thought, and narrative voice.

450
00:25:35,440 --> 00:25:38,700
Your first draft will often contain more telling than showing.

451
00:25:38,700 --> 00:25:42,440
During revisions, you can go through and highlight parts that need more flavour.

452
00:25:42,440 --> 00:25:44,940
Look for spots where emotions or descriptions feel vague.

453
00:25:44,940 --> 00:25:46,300
Like your�

454
00:25:46,400 --> 00:25:48,880
Or place those with specific sensory details and vivid vocabulary.

455
00:25:48,880 --> 00:25:52,060
If you want to learn more about, if you want to learn more about telling vs showing, I highly recommend

456
00:25:53,440 --> 00:25:55,440
Jannes Hardie's Understanding Show Don't Tell.

457
00:25:55,440 --> 00:25:59,980
It's a short read that's jam-packed with practical strategies for finding red flags with telling.

458
00:26:00,640 --> 00:26:05,000
As a writing exercise, find a short paragraph or scene from one of your favorite books,

459
00:26:05,400 --> 00:26:07,180
and replace all the showing with telling.

460
00:26:07,600 --> 00:26:10,100
Remember the passage I shared at the beginning of this video?

461
00:26:10,500 --> 00:26:14,040
That's actually from a popular novel, except I rewrote it badly.

462
00:26:14,640 --> 00:26:19,060
Here's the original text, which is an admirable example of showing a character's emotions

463
00:26:19,060 --> 00:26:21,860
through her actions and a unique narrative voice.

464
00:26:22,280 --> 00:26:25,320
It's from Where the Crawdads Sing by Delilah Owens.

465
00:26:26,340 --> 00:26:31,840
Sometimes she heard night sounds she didn't know, or jumped from lightning too close.

466
00:26:32,760 --> 00:26:36,360
But whenever she stumbled, it was the land that caught her.

467
00:26:37,400 --> 00:26:45,140
Until at last, at some unclaimed moment, the heart pain seeped away like water into sand.

468
00:26:46,260 --> 00:26:47,900
Still there, but deep.

469
00:26:48,960 --> 00:26:54,780
Kaya laid her hand upon the breathing wet earth, and the marsh became her mother.

470
00:26:55,440 --> 00:26:57,440
She was a little girl, but she was a little girl.

471
00:26:58,260 --> 00:27:00,260
Do you struggle to show instead of tell?

472
00:27:00,860 --> 00:27:02,860
Share your thoughts with me in the comments.

473
00:27:03,640 --> 00:27:05,640
Whatever you do, keep writing.

474
00:27:06,780 --> 00:27:08,780
Thanks for watching!

475
00:27:08,780 --> 00:27:10,780
